  "strategy": "## Approach and Rationale\n\nWe aim to minimize the overlap integral C\u2085 through an iterative optimization approach. The problem is nonconvex, so we use a heuristic method combining gradient descent with constraint projections to find a good local minimum. Starting from the initial construction, the algorithm alternates between gradient descent steps and feasibility projection to maintain the problem constraints.\n\n## Mathematical Specification\n\n1. **Variables**:\n   - $h \\in [0,1]^{n \\times 1}$: Discretized function values\n   - $n$: Number of samples in discretization\n\n2. **Objective**:\n   - Minimize $C_5 = \\max_k \\left( \\text{overlap}_k(h) \\right)$\n   - Where $\\text{overlap}_k(h) = \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} h[i](1 - h[(i + k) \\mod n]) \\cdot dx$\n   - We use a smooth approximation for the maximum: $\\log(\\sum \\exp(\\text{overlap}_k(h)/\\epsilon))$\n\n3. **Constraints**:\n   - $0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1$ for all $i$\n   - $\\sum h[i] \\cdot dx = 1$\n   - Implemented via projection step after gradient updates\n\n4. **Solver**:\n   - No numerical solver. Instead, use projected gradient descent\n   - Gradient computed via finite differences\n\n## Implementation Procedure\n\n### Step 1: Problem Setup\n- **Inputs**:\n  - Initial `h_values` or derive from random initialization\n  - `n_points`: Discretization resolution\n- **Setup**:\n  - Define `n_points` (e.g., 500 for a balance between accuracy and speed)\n  - Initialize `h = initial_h_values` or create a new initialization\n\n### Step 2: Optimization Loop\n- **Initialize**:\n  - Set learning rate $\\alpha$ (e.g., 0.01)\n  - Define smoothness parameter $\\epsilon$ (e.g., 0.1)\n  - Initialize best_h and best_c5\n- **Loop until time budget exhausted**:\n  - Compute objective function: $C_5^{approx} = \\log(\\sum \\exp(\\text{overlap}_k / \\epsilon)) \\cdot dx$\n  - Compute gradient $\\nabla C_5^{approx}$ via finite differences\n  - Update: $h = h - \\alpha \\cdot \\nabla C_5^{approx}$\n  - Project back to feasible region: $\\text{clip}(h, 0, 1)$\n  - Rescale to maintain integral: $h = \\text{normalize}(h)$\n  - Keep track of the best solution (lowest C\u2085)\n\n### Step 3: Validation\n- After projection, validate constraints:\n  - Ensure $\\sum h[i] \\cdot dx = 1$ within tolerance\n  - Check $0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1$\n\n### Step 4: Termination\n- Track time spent. When budget nearly exhausted, perform a final exact evaluation of the best solution found.\n\n## Search Choices and Variations\n- **Initialization**:\n  - Use `initial_h_values`\n  - Try random initializations with enforced integral constraint\n- **Learning rate**:\n  - Start with $\\alpha = 0.01$, decrease by factor 0.9 every 100 iterations\n- **Smoothness parameter**:\n  - Start with $\\epsilon = 0.1$, adaptive adjustment based on progress\n- **Discretization**:\n  - Try multiple resolutions (e.g., 200, 500, 1000 points) and select based on achieved C\u2085\n\n## Compute Budget and Robustness\n- **Time Estimation**:\n  - Gradient computation dominates. Expect 1e-4 seconds per gradient step.\n  - With 1000 iterations, total time ~0.1 seconds, leaving plenty of room.\n- **Robustness**:\n  - Use restarts if progress stalls\n  - Always maintain a valid solution in case of failure\n  - Track best solution so far to ensure return even if optimization fails\n\n## Validation and Return\n- Check feasibility: $h \\in [0,1]^{n_points}$ and $\\sum h \\cdot dx = 1$\n- Compute exact C\u2085 using the provided evaluation method\n- Ensure C\u2085 improves upon the initial value\n- Return `h`, `C\u2085`, and `n_points`",
